the niger delta is at the heart of  nigeria's oil industry  for which it has paid a price  environmental contamination  on a vast scale the all giant shell and  its nigerian subsidiary have always  denied any responsibility  but today a dutch court disagreed  siding with four nigerian farmers who  say their livelihoods were destroyed  by the leaks today justice  has prevailed we thank god and  we are still we are still calling  ourselves to come and do the necessary  so that livelihood will return back so  that we will live fine  their road to justice has been long it  began 13 years ago and since then two of  the farmers have  died including prince will's father ali  i'm very happy i'm very very happy yes  i'm very happy because  right now the common man in nigeria now  has hope  the case centers on two spills between  2004 and 2007  covering an area equivalent to 60  football pitches  the farmers filed their case in 2008 in  the netherlands  where royal dutch shell is headquartered  the company says the leaks were caused  by sabotage with both  sides appealing a ruling in 2013 but  today shell's nigerian subsidiary  was found responsible in order to pay  compensation  how much is to be decided for  environmentalists it is a key victory  those who live in the delta paint a  bleak picture to live  another data for short is to live in  hell  he gives hope to others whose livelihood  have been destroyed completely  it gives them hope that no matter how  long it takes  that justice will come for them one day  shell has said it's disappointed by the  ruling which could have ramifications  beyond nigeria  breaking new ground on how far  multinationals can be held  responsible for their actions overseas  it will open  maybe the gates for people to sue but i  think it will also mean that parent  companies will exercise  better control over their subsidiaries  and ensure  that best practices are put in place in  the same way as they do  in their home states  shell can still appeal the ruling so  this lengthy battle may yet continue  but for now those living in the niger  delta say they have hope  iva bennett sky news
 

